00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:42.750
I turn 70 soon.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.390
What is your best advice to keep
my brain healthy in old age?

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.680
Terrific question.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:50.310
The advice I would give to you,
as somebody about to reach 70,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.900
is the same advice
I give to anybody,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.160
which is that, essentially,
all of the things that improve

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.250
cardiovascular
health and perfusion

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.210
of your bodily tissues are
going to improve functionality

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.340
of the brain.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:11.970
Because, of course, the brain
is a rich consumer of fuel,

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:15.660
requires very good portals
to deliver those fuels,

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.450
and the capillaries, micro
capillaries and arteries,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.250
and so forth, need to
be clean and clear.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.070
That's the big one.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.700
This is why I think
the prescription now

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.070
is that's generally
accepted, and here I'm

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:29.250
borrowing from my friend
Peter Attia, but about 150,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.940
or maybe as much as 200 minutes
of so-called zone 2 cardio

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.460
per week, movement that you
can just barely carry out

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.670
a conversation, is
going to be very useful.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.170
One thing that's
often not discussed

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.203
is that load bearing
exercise of some sort

00:03:42.203 --> 00:03:44.620
is going to be better, provided
your body can tolerate it,

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.430
but you should do something
that you can do consistently

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:51.510
over those long durations
without injuring yourself.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.280
But there's a very
interesting literature

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.910
about how load bearing
movements actually

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:58.510
generate the
release of hormones,

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:00.720
yes, hormones, from
bone that actually

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.600
cross the blood-brain
barrier and may influence

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:07.110
health of neurons in brain
areas such as the hippocampus.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.260
And there, I'm extending from
preclinical data in animals

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.930
to humans, but there's
some human data starting

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:14.320
to emerge that that's true.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.720
It's also true, and there's
a wonderful paper out

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.130
just today or yesterday
from Dr. Andy Galpin's lab

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.800
and collaborators
talking about how

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.630
if you look at
cognitive health, it's

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.968
highly correlated with things
that relate to strength.

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:30.760
And that is not to say
that you should just

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:32.350
do strength training
exercises but we

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.390
know that all people,
truly all people,

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.390
should be doing some sort
of resistance training

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.710
two or three times per week.

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.830
You know, and we know that
grip strength and increasing

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:45.190
asymmetry in grip strength
between the two hands

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:48.490
is one of the indicators
of deficits in control

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:50.320
from the brain out
to the periphery,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.270
and it's correlated
with cognitive decline.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.190
There's also some
interesting data

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:58.240
about how when the feet become
floppy and kind of flaccid

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.180
or the lack of ability
to extend one's toes.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.430
I'm still working, I've been
wearing this toe spreader

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:03.670
thing.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:04.780
Has anyone tried those?

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:05.560
Those hurt.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:06.152
Those hurt.

00:05:06.152 --> 00:05:07.610
I broke this foot
a bunch of times.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.760
but I'll tell you when you get
better at spreading your toes,

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.020
it's really exciting.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:13.090
And it's really exciting
for several reasons.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.590
It's really exciting
because there's

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:15.757
more stability in your feet.

00:05:15.757 --> 00:05:18.340
You can run and move and do
things better without pain.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.100
But in addition to that,
believe it or not, just as one

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.030
of the first things
that they're going

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.050
to do when you come
into this world

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.800
is scrape the bottom
of your foot and look

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.270
for the Babinski reflex,
which is a neural transmission

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:33.223
reflex, as all reflexes
are, but it's testing,

00:05:33.223 --> 00:05:35.140
essentially, the health
of the nervous system.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:38.762
That over time, again,
there are many correlates

00:05:38.762 --> 00:05:40.720
of dementia, many, many
correlates of dementia,

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.080
but an inability to finely
control the extremities

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.260
is certainly one of them.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:48.040
So strength training,
cardiovascular training, these

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.300
are kind of stereotyped
answers for your question

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:54.820
and, yet, those are
really the prime movers

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:57.970
against cardiovascular and
cerebrovascular disease.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:00.305
And then, of course,
I'd be remiss

00:06:00.305 --> 00:06:02.680
if I didn't throw in something
that was a little bit more

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.980
edgy because that's what I do.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:08.860
There are interesting
data about the use

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.630
of drugs to increase
acetylcholine transmission,

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:12.130
right?

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:16.260
I mean, I was visiting a
Nobel Prize winner at Columbia

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.020
to learn about his incredible
work some years ago

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:21.330
and saw that he chewed
no fewer than five pieces

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.520
of Nicorette gum, something
I don't recommend,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.470
during this short meeting.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.017
And I said, what
is this all about?

00:06:27.017 --> 00:06:29.100
And he said, well, you
know, I don't smoke anymore

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.475
because I don't
want lung cancer.

00:06:30.475 --> 00:06:34.233
But he said nicotine
is protective against

00:06:34.233 --> 00:06:35.400
Parkinson's and Alzheimer's.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:36.840
I was like, how can that be?

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.200
And he said, well, you know,
decreases in neuromodulation,

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.938
dopamine, acetylcholine
correlated

00:06:41.938 --> 00:06:43.980
with cognitive decline,
keeping your brain sharp,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.190
and so on and so forth.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:47.670
So I'm not encouraging
people to take nicotine.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:50.700
It increases blood
pressure, vasoconstriction,

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.220
but it's an interesting
consideration

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.990
some of the emerging cholinergic
and dopaminergic drugs are ways

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.850
to increase acetylcholine
and dopamine

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:01.260
are certainly intriguing.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.940
And I won't tell you
who that person is

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:04.380
but his name is Richard Axel.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.550
Next question.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:06.690
Yeah.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.330
How can I optimize sleep
while working 24/7 hour

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.080
shifts as a firefighter,
24 hours on 48 hours off?

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:13.830
OK.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.230
And this probably also
pertains to new parents,

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:19.540
and it probably also
pertains to anyone

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:22.270
that's going through a
particularly stressful time,

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:24.550
where you're micro waking
throughout the night, so not

00:07:24.550 --> 00:07:25.510
just firefighters.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:26.390
So what do we know?

00:07:26.390 --> 00:07:29.680
We know, based on
really good data,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.670
that shift work is bad for us.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:32.710
It's just bad.

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:34.390
We're a diurnal species.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:35.350
We're not nocturnal.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:38.800
But thank you, thank you,
thank you, shift workers,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.990
because you essentially
keep us all safe

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.670
and make the world go round.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.950
And so we need you, and
we want you healthy.

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:47.890
So one of the main
things is that you

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:52.540
can make sure that you stay on
the same sleep-wake schedule,

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:54.970
excuse me, for at
least two weeks.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:59.440
It's the swing shift
that's really the worst.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.420
You can tell your
boss I said that.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:04.570
And if they won't
agree, and you're

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:06.428
doing this 24 hour
on 24 hour off,

00:08:06.428 --> 00:08:08.720
there are a couple of things
that are really important.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.830
First of all, the main way to
wake up your nervous system,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.030
even though it might not
feel like a triple espresso

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.798
is going to be that light
exposure to the eyes.

00:08:18.798 --> 00:08:20.340
And if you can't
get it from sunlight

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.700
is going to be from any
bright, artificial light.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.910
I'm not a huge proponent
of the daylight simulators.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:27.900
They're very expensive.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:31.797
You can simply buy a 900 lux
led far more inexpensively.

00:08:31.797 --> 00:08:33.630
I don't have any
relationship to any company

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:36.179
that sells these but you
can find them on Amazon

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:39.630
or wherever you happen to
prefer to purchase things.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.570
Or you can just get really
close to a bright light.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:44.190
Anytime you're
trying to wake up,

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.650
even if you don't feel
that it helps you wake up

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.180
very much, mostly
for the melatonin

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.070
suppression, because
bright light will very

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.690
acutely suppress melatonin.

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.150
And then the real
question from shiftworkers

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.790
always seems to be,
should I catch up on sleep

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:58.650
or is that going
to be problematic?

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:00.910
Should I just stay up
into the next cycle?

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:03.150
And the answer there is
a little bit nuanced,

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.850
but the best answer I
can give across the board

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.980
is, if this is a
pattern that you're

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:11.190
going to be in regularly,
over say months or years,

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:13.860
then get whatever sleep you can.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.180
Get whatever sleep you can.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.388
If it's something that you're
doing somewhat acutely,

00:09:17.388 --> 00:09:19.347
like you're traveling to
Europe and you're just

00:09:19.347 --> 00:09:21.570
going to force yourself to
stay up a day and a half

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:25.470
then, in that case,
I would say, no need

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:28.260
to get the maximum
amount of sleep.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.300
Just try and stay with
the local schedule.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:32.880
We have an entire
episode about shift work

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.350
that somehow maybe didn't
get as much recognition

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.147
as it should have
for shift workers,

00:09:39.147 --> 00:09:40.980
and we'll try and get
it out in better form.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.770
We don't always succeed
in top carding things

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.060
in a way that gets them
out to the most people.

