00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.410
Next question, please.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.240
How does hypnosis therapy work?

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:22.930
Well, this is a very
interesting topic to me

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.560
because my colleague,
Associate Chair of Psychiatry

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:32.200
at Stanford, David Spiegel,
is a world expert in hypnosis

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.360
and its neural
underpinnings and its use

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:36.190
for clinical applications.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:40.930
His father was a hypnotist,
also a psychiatrist.

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:44.860
And when people hear hypnotism,
they think of stage hypnotism

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.988
and being up on stage and
doing things you don't want to

00:10:47.988 --> 00:10:49.030
in front of other people.

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:50.620
But really, it's
when we're talking

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:53.285
about clinical
applications or wellness

00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:54.910
applications of
hypnosis, we're talking

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:56.800
about self-directed hypnosis.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.450
I really wish there
was a better name,

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:00.550
because I don't think
hypnosis is going

00:11:00.550 --> 00:11:03.100
to advance very far as a field,
frankly, because everyone

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:05.200
thinks hypnosis.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:07.150
And it would be
like if psychedelics

00:11:07.150 --> 00:11:09.550
were just called drugs, right?

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:12.310
We were taught in the
80s that drugs are bad,

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.560
and that your brain on
drugs looks like an omelet,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.010
and that's bad.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.490
And if you like omelets,
they're still bad.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:21.250
And drugs of abuse are bad.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.085
And, actually, I hope
we can talk briefly

00:11:23.085 --> 00:11:24.460
about psychedelics
at some point,

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:25.510
because I do think
there's a little bit

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:28.030
of a runaway train around the
topic of psychedelics now.

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.760
I think we need to be
very careful how we

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:32.710
approach that entire landscape.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.250
But hypnosis essentially
works by allowing someone

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:39.310
to place their own brain
into this very unique state.

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:41.350
Earlier, we were talking
about neuroplasticity,

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:45.940
and we talked about the fact
that neuroplasticity involves

00:11:45.940 --> 00:11:49.240
intense focus followed by deep
rest in the form of deep sleep

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.330
or non-sleep deep rest, maybe
even Rick Rubining it and just

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:53.710
kind of like laying there.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:58.320
Hypnosis is different
because hypnosis

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:03.000
is a state in which your
focus is very narrow,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.780
the context is very narrow,
but you're very, very relaxed.

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:10.290
So maybe the Rubin example of
being brain active and body

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:15.330
very still is a bit more
like hypnosis, to be fair.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:18.960
Why would it be the case that
David Spiegel and his dad

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.240
have literally a tool that is
approved by the Psychiatric

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.300
Association, the major American
Psychiatric Association,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.130
where they can figure
out how hypnotizable

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:31.472
you are by having look
up and try and close

00:12:31.472 --> 00:12:33.180
your eyelids while
continuing to look up,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.830
the so-called Spiegel
eye roll test.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:36.300
Sounds pretty wacky, right?

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:39.340
This is like TikTok level wacky.

00:12:39.340 --> 00:12:41.480
Well, the reason is you
have cranial nerves,

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.310
so they sit more or less
near your neck, that

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.070
allow you to direct your
focus, your eyes, upward,

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.980
and then you have cranial nerves
that have your eyes go down

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:53.850
and the ones that the cranial
nerves that drive your eyes up

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:57.030
are associated with
alertness and eyes open.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:57.710
No surprise.

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:59.460
And the cranial nerves
that are associated

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.280
with pointing your eyes down
and closing your eyelids

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.753
are associated with, what,
with drowsiness, sleep,

00:13:04.753 --> 00:13:05.670
and lack of alertness.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.490
There's sort of a push-pull in
the autonomic nervous system.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.270
And Spiegel, Spiegel's
daddy and him,

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:15.690
figured out, because
they're geniuses,

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.330
that if somebody
can maintain upward

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.830
gaze while closing their
eyelids, two things happen.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:22.620
One, you'll see the
whites of their eyes,

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:24.220
and it's pretty creepy.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.770
Two, that means they're
highly hypnotizable,

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.740
because that is a reflection
of the probability

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.810
that they can enter a
brain state in which they

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.170
are both very awake
and very relaxed.

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:36.340
Pretty cool.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.230
Now, if that sounds
kind of wacky,

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.105
because you're just
looking at the periphery,

00:13:40.105 --> 00:13:42.250
keep in mind, that one
of the primary entry

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:46.540
points for diagnosing concussion
is to shine a light in one eye

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:48.820
and have that pupil
constrict and then

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:51.010
see whether or not the
other pupil constricts,

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:54.085
the so-called consensual
pupillary reflex.

00:13:54.085 --> 00:13:55.960
Although, technically,
and I've been bothered

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:57.460
by this from day
one, it should be

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.830
called the non-consensual
pupil reflex,

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.410
because the other eye doesn't
have a choice if everything's

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:03.100
working.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:05.480
[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:09.622
In any case, if you have
a hard hit to the head,

00:14:09.622 --> 00:14:11.830
you'll see that you shine
light in one eye, the pupil

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.020
constricts, and the other
one stays really dilated.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.677
And then you go, OK, get this
person to the emergency room

00:14:16.677 --> 00:14:18.760
because there's been a
severing of the connections

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:20.218
between the two
sides of the brain.

00:14:20.218 --> 00:14:22.280
So looking in the
eyes and trying

00:14:22.280 --> 00:14:24.280
to deduce what might be
happening more centrally

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.830
within the caverns of
the skull and the brain

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:28.090
is not a new thing.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:31.000
It is a primary diagnostic
tool in neurology.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.610
It's also how your parents
knew that you were taking drugs

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.693
when you came in the door
because your pupils were

00:14:35.693 --> 00:14:39.040
like that big, and that
reflects a difference

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.600
in autonomic arousal.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:44.500
And, basically, stimulants,
as, most dilate their pupils.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.140
This is also why the story
about belladonna, people

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:48.845
intentionally
dilating their pupils

00:14:48.845 --> 00:14:50.470
to trick people into
thinking that they

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:51.940
were attracted to them.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.460
I've thought about
this one a lot, too.

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:57.760
It's like not a precursor
to good relationship.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:00.160
It's like someone's
using their physiology

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:01.570
to pretend that
they're attracted

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.430
so the other person thinks that
they're attracted so that they

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:04.390
might become attracted.

00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:06.580
Anyway, it's a recipe for
failure, almost as bad

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:07.940
as most of the dating apps.

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:08.857
Well, I wouldn't know.

00:15:08.857 --> 00:15:10.700
I'm not one them,
but from what I hear.

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:11.840
OK.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:13.550
So where were we?

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:14.990
Hypnosis.

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:19.170
When you are in a state
of elevated attention

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:22.410
but very relaxed, guess
what, neuroplasticity

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.660
occurs much faster
because you're essentially

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:27.690
marrying the two states
that are normally divorced,

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.150
which are heightened
levels of attention

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:31.770
first and then deep rest.

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.927
You're essentially putting the
nervous system into a more,

00:15:34.927 --> 00:15:37.260
I wouldn't call it hypoplastic
state, but a more plastic

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:37.760
state.

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:40.050
And for people that are
highly hypnotizable,

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:45.200
the success rates at, for
instance, smoking cessation,

00:15:45.200 --> 00:15:48.677
pain relief, are
pretty impressive.

00:15:48.677 --> 00:15:50.510
Spiegel Lab has published
a number of these.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:53.330
So I think self hypnosis
is a very interesting tool.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:55.040
I just hope that
they rename it so

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.660
that it stands a chance
of getting off the ground.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:15:59.077
I mean, one of the
things that you

00:15:59.077 --> 00:16:01.010
learn as a public
facing educator

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:04.280
is that what things are
called has a great impact on

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:09.650
whether or not they achieve
any kind of use in the world.

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:15.500
Hence, why I decided to swallow
the difficult pill of partially

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:18.350
renaming yoga nidra as
non-sleep deep rest.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:19.670
I don't like to do that.

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:22.010
Yoga nidra has more
than 1,000 year history.

00:16:22.010 --> 00:16:25.460
But when people hear yoga
nidra, unless they are very open

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:27.880
minded, they hear magic carpet.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.007
They hear levitation,
and it's unfortunate,

00:16:30.007 --> 00:16:31.090
and that's not how I feel.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:33.260
But for years, I talked about
yoga nidra, it's so cool.

00:16:33.260 --> 00:16:35.510
It's like a sleep state but
it's-- they're like, yeah,

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:36.670
like yoga nidra.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:37.750
OK.

00:16:37.750 --> 00:16:40.220
But if you come from a culture
where that's discussed,

00:16:40.220 --> 00:16:41.330
they're all about it.

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.890
And so non-sleep deep rest, you
know, I felt like, all right,

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:46.053
leave my name out of it.

00:16:46.053 --> 00:16:47.470
You know, I'll be
dead eventually.

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:49.303
I mean, I'm in this
line of advisors, right?

00:16:49.303 --> 00:16:50.920
I'm like approaching 50.

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:53.560
I'm like, I'm winning
in my lineage.

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:57.880
But should I be fortunate enough
to live past bullet cancer

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:04.238
or car crash far enough, then
NSDR hopefully will persist,

00:17:04.238 --> 00:17:05.530
and I don't need a piece of it.

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:06.670
It's just the hope
is that people

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:08.500
will learn to put themselves
into brain states that

00:17:08.500 --> 00:17:10.390
can be adaptive for
them so it'd be nice

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:13.390
if someone could come up with
something other than hypnosis.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:14.890
I think Spiegel would agree.

00:17:14.890 --> 00:17:17.530
Super interested in
psychedelics as medicine

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:19.540
to be done with somebody
with experience.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:21.730
Worried about unlocking
mental health conditions.

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:22.510
Yeah.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:23.470
You should be.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.600
What does the research say,
and what are your thoughts?

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:27.100
OK.

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:34.850
Barbed wire question,
we like that.

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:38.180
Psychedelics, well, let's
just back up a little bit

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:41.240
and acknowledge one
thing that's more

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:44.202
important than
psychedelics or anything

00:17:44.202 --> 00:17:46.160
else when it comes to
rewiring the brain, which

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:51.290
is that, ultimately, rewiring
of the brain is about shifts

00:17:51.290 --> 00:17:54.500
in neuromodulators, dopamine,
serotonin, epinephrine,

00:17:54.500 --> 00:17:56.330
norepinephrine, acetylcholine.

00:17:56.330 --> 00:18:01.670
And it's no coincidence that
SSRIs, Selective Serotonin

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:05.060
Reuptake Inhibitors, have
been one of the major entry

00:18:05.060 --> 00:18:08.090
points for attempts to
treat things like depression

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:11.060
over the last 20-30
years or more.

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:13.490
When I was in college, that's
when the book Listening

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:14.300
to Prozac came out.

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.008
But prior to that,
there's a long history

00:18:16.008 --> 00:18:19.370
of drugs, prescription
drugs, in that case,

00:18:19.370 --> 00:18:21.350
to change levels
of neuromodulators,

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:23.960
like serotonin or
acetylcholine or dopamine,

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:27.380
in an attempt to cure
or treat a disease.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:32.450
But keep in mind, despite
the varied success of SSRIs,

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:35.812
a topic unto itself, that
there's a strong belief,

00:18:35.812 --> 00:18:37.520
and there has been
for a long time, that,

00:18:37.520 --> 00:18:41.840
if SSRIs worked it wasn't
because the depressed brain is

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.430
deficient in
serotonin, but rather

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:45.950
because increasing serotonin
offered the opportunity

00:18:45.950 --> 00:18:48.320
to increase neuroplasticity.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.420
So there's a different
way of thinking about it,

00:18:50.420 --> 00:18:53.480
and that's a segue for saying
that when you say psychedelics,

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.520
that's a broad
category of drugs.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:58.013
Nowadays, people even
lump ketamine into that.

00:18:58.013 --> 00:18:59.930
Although, technically,
it's not a psychedelic.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:01.880
But the sort of
classic, if you will,

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:05.240
psychedelics are LSD,
Lysergic Acid Diethylamide,

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:08.330
and psilocybin, aka mushrooms.

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:10.770
Although, it comes in
other sources, as well.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.040
The major effect
of psilocybin is

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:15.380
to stimulate a
particular serotonin

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:19.580
receptor which has elevated
density in particular brain

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:20.120
areas.

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:26.780
And indeed, there are many
recent clinical trials, many,

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:32.690
let's say 12 to 20 good
sized clinical trials done

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:36.590
in diverse locations on the
planet, many at Johns Hopkins

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:41.030
and UCSF, some in Switzerland,
showing that enhancing,

00:19:41.030 --> 00:19:44.180
and here you'll notice I'm
using the mechanistic language,

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:48.440
enhancing the transmission
of the release of serotonin

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:50.990
and activating particular
serotonin receptors

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:55.790
leads to an opportunity for
more, what, neuroplasticity.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:20:00.230
Now, I say it that way not
to add a bunch of word soup

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:03.290
but because the real
question is whether or not

00:20:03.290 --> 00:20:05.660
the experience
that one has while

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:07.970
under the influence
of psychedelics

00:20:07.970 --> 00:20:13.550
is critical to the
clinical outcome.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:17.390
Or a growing idea,
just as a hypothesis,

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:18.770
I think is equally
interesting is

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:23.150
that it's the serotonin itself
and that the things you see,

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:25.190
the things you hear, the
things you experience

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:26.600
are not relevant.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:31.610
Now, I have some experience
with psychedelics.

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:34.477
I had a bad time on
psychedelics as a teenager.

00:20:34.477 --> 00:20:35.810
I was also pretty wayward youth.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:38.243
But I've had a bad trip on LSD.

00:20:38.243 --> 00:20:40.160
For years, I was scared
that people were going

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:41.780
to dose me with LSD after that.

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:43.410
It was so bad.

00:20:43.410 --> 00:20:46.970
I think across the board, we
can say, kids doing psychedelics

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:48.170
just seems like a bad idea.

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:49.970
Their brains are
already hypoplastic.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:56.020
If you have a predisposition
to bipolar type

00:20:56.020 --> 00:21:01.060
issues or schizophrenia, it
can exacerbate those issues.

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.733
And, certainly, if you
don't have adequate support,

00:21:03.733 --> 00:21:06.400
in the form of somebody that can
guide you through the sessions,

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:08.950
as well as the
presessions, which are not

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:12.310
done with psychedelics, as well
as the so-called integration

00:21:12.310 --> 00:21:15.010
afterwards, it can be a
really slippery slope.

00:21:15.010 --> 00:21:17.770
I know examples of
people really suffering

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:19.570
in the aftermath of
psychedelic journeys.

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:21.740
Now, there are a few
interesting points, as well,

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:25.100
and it wouldn't be fair if
I didn't say that several,

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:27.950
if not many,
individuals who have

00:21:27.950 --> 00:21:31.215
had so-called treatment
resistant depression, at least

00:21:31.215 --> 00:21:32.840
in these clinical
trials, have reported

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:37.040
feeling far better after
psilocybin therapy,

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:41.030
but that psilocybin therapy was
done with several presessions,

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:44.298
then the psychedelic sessions
then several after sessions.

00:21:44.298 --> 00:21:46.590
And it's not always the case
that things turn out well.

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:48.920
So I think it's early days.

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.020
What is interesting,
and I think important,

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:55.100
is to recognize that psilocybin
and the structure of psilocybin

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:59.540
is very similar to serotonin
itself, very similar,

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:01.490
but it activates
particular receptors.

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:04.100
A lot of people don't realize
how similar to serotonin it is,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:06.830
and that microdosing
psilocybin, I

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:08.270
should say the
data on microdosing

00:22:08.270 --> 00:22:10.970
psilocybin, something
that's increasingly popular,

00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:14.970
is not particularly compelling.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:16.820
It's not clear what it does.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:20.560
It's not clear if
it's of any use.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:23.060
And I think the danger here is
that we end up in a situation

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:24.985
as we did with,
frankly, with cannabis.

00:22:24.985 --> 00:22:26.360
And, by the way,
I'm not somebody

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:27.530
who demonizes cannabis.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:29.840
I think it has its uses
for certain people,

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:32.180
but very high THC
concentration cannabis

00:22:32.180 --> 00:22:34.550
can be a problem, especially
with people that have

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:36.110
a predisposition to psychosis.

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:39.050
And anyone that tells you
that cannabis isn't addictive,

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:42.270
just say, great, don't
smoke weed for a week.

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:44.050
Let's see how you do.

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:44.590
Yeah.

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:47.290
And let's go on a
plane trip together.

00:22:47.290 --> 00:22:48.640
And how are you sleeping?

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:53.588
And so I think the chronic
cannabis users are starting

00:22:53.588 --> 00:22:55.630
to take note of some of
the issues it causes but,

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:58.120
again, there are some
clinical applications.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:02.020
Now, when it comes to the
high speed train psychedelics,

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:07.040
like DMT, that's far less
data available there.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:09.105
And then MDMA,
assisted psychotherapy

00:23:09.105 --> 00:23:10.480
for the treatment
of PTSD, there,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:12.350
the data I think
are more robust,

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:14.260
and I think we're likely
to see legalization

00:23:14.260 --> 00:23:17.140
or at least decriminalization
in the next few years.

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:23.080
But keep in mind that MDMA is
methylenedioxymethamphetamine.

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:26.440
So for people that like
dopaminergic states,

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:28.090
it's a particularly
compelling state

00:23:28.090 --> 00:23:31.107
to be in, so much so that they
could overindulge in MDMA,

00:23:31.107 --> 00:23:32.690
and then there's the
issues of purity,

00:23:32.690 --> 00:23:35.330
and I could do a five hour
podcast on this right now.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.350
So I think the important point
is approach with caution.

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:40.910
Kids, absolutely not.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:44.930
And I think it's an exciting
landscape, very exciting,

00:23:44.930 --> 00:23:47.660
and whereas a
discussion like the one

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:52.020
we just had would have gotten
me fired a few years ago.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:53.990
I mean, Stanford has
big programs now,

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:55.640
a lot of philanthropy,
federal grants,

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:59.570
and many laboratories focused
on the study of psychedelics.

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:02.660
So I would say stay tuned.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:06.440
But keep in mind that increasing
neuromodulator levels very

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:09.110
acutely, whether or not it's
with a prescription drug

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:11.060
or whether or not it's
with psychedelics,

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:14.960
is really what lies at
the heart of the recovery,

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:16.550
the potential
recovery, I should say,

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:18.290
or the negative
effects that happen

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:22.790
to occur in anyone that embarks
on the psychedelic journey.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.412
Do people who meditate and
need less sleep than people who

00:24:25.412 --> 00:24:26.276
don't?

00:24:26.276 --> 00:24:28.680
Oh, that's interesting.

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:33.900
Well, we know that from a study
by Wendy Suzuki, who I believe,

00:24:33.900 --> 00:24:36.060
if NYU made the
right choice, and I

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:39.360
think they did is, now the Dean
of Arts and Sciences at NYU.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:41.790
She ran a memory
lab for a long time.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.580
She has data showing
that even 10, I

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:48.760
think it's 13, but as little
as 10 minutes of meditation,

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:51.180
so it'll be sitting
still, breathing,

00:24:51.180 --> 00:24:53.730
focusing on your breathing,
directing one's attention

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:55.680
to third eye center, et cetera.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.080
We don't have a third eye.

00:24:57.080 --> 00:24:58.830
The pineal is thought
to be the third eye.

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:01.020
But I don't know
why people say it's

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.970
a light sensitive
tissue deep in the brain

00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:04.970
but maybe that's why they
call it the third eye.

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:06.630
But, in any case,
that type of practice

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:11.110
has been shown to increase
memory focus, aka learning,

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:13.020
but there's some
interesting footnotes

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:15.570
in those papers, which
point to the fact

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:18.780
that when people meditate too
close to bedtime, oftentimes,

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:21.600
they have trouble sleeping
because, basically, meditation

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:23.940
is a focusing exercise.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:25.928
It's a perceptual exercise.

00:25:25.928 --> 00:25:27.970
I don't think of meditation
is anything mystical.

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:30.670
It's a self-directed shift
in your perception to,

00:25:30.670 --> 00:25:33.580
what, to your interoception,
to your internal state,

00:25:33.580 --> 00:25:36.050
as opposed to anything beyond
the confines of your skin.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:38.092
There's nothing
mystical about that.

00:25:38.092 --> 00:25:39.550
And then, in that
state, your brain

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:42.988
starts to generate
patterns of activity that

00:25:42.988 --> 00:25:44.530
are distinct from
when you're sharing

00:25:44.530 --> 00:25:46.572
your attention between
what's going on internally

00:25:46.572 --> 00:25:49.270
and what's happening
out in the world, right?

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:51.460
I think we need to
demystify what people

00:25:51.460 --> 00:25:52.648
have cloaked as mystical.

00:25:52.648 --> 00:25:54.190
And when I say
cloaked, I don't think

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:56.500
that the people that have
meditated for thousands

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:00.843
of years thought that there
was anything mystical about it

00:26:00.843 --> 00:26:02.260
but, sometimes,
what we experience

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.090
there can feel mystical.

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:06.280
So if you have
trouble sleeping, I

00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:10.390
recommend doing some sort of
non-sleep deep rest practice,

00:26:10.390 --> 00:26:13.090
like NSDR, aka yoga nidra.

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.430
Although, those are different.

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:19.720
NSDR generally lacks
the intention piece.

00:26:19.720 --> 00:26:22.672
And the ones that I've
put into the world,

00:26:22.672 --> 00:26:24.130
we've stripped away
the intentions,

00:26:24.130 --> 00:26:26.797
and we've stripped away any kind
of language that would make you

00:26:26.797 --> 00:26:31.480
think that there
was some sort of,

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.940
let's just say, like
cultural aspect to it,

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:37.650
which, again, is admittedly
a bit unfair to the origin

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:38.760
practice of yoga nidra.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:40.792
But the problem, again,
is that in yoga nidra,

00:26:40.792 --> 00:26:42.750
you're going to be doing
intentions and hearing

00:26:42.750 --> 00:26:44.850
language that for
some people, not all,

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:48.030
might divorce you from
the wish to do it.

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:52.115
In any case, non-sleep deep
rest done at any time of day,

00:26:52.115 --> 00:26:53.490
but especially if
you fall asleep

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:54.870
in the middle of
the night, is going

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:56.460
to be useful for helping
you fall back asleep.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:57.918
Whereas meditation,
again, is going

00:26:57.918 --> 00:27:00.240
to enhance your level of focus.

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:03.450
So I don't think
it's a good practice

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:05.010
if you have trouble sleeping.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:08.280
Now, to finally answer your
question, if you meditate,

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.350
can you afford to sleep less?

00:27:10.350 --> 00:27:12.630
My friend, Matt
Walker, would say no.

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:16.170
However, many of us can't
sleep as much as we want to,

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:18.750
and many of us are not like
Matt, where we can wake up

00:27:18.750 --> 00:27:20.490
without an alarm clock.

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:22.440
I'll just keep
sleeping and sleeping,

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.172
unless I went to
bed at like 8:00.

00:27:24.172 --> 00:27:25.380
This is actually interesting.

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:27.090
There's an asymmetry
to your sleep needs.

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:29.740
If you go to bed,
remember that old adage,

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:32.283
every hour before midnight
is worth two after?

00:27:32.283 --> 00:27:33.700
Well, it turns out
that for people

00:27:33.700 --> 00:27:36.670
that are meant to be early
risers, going to bed at 8:00,

00:27:36.670 --> 00:27:38.590
you'll wake up at 3:00
or 4:00 feeling great.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.960
You go to bed at
11:00, you feel groggy,

00:27:40.960 --> 00:27:43.090
and there are good
reasons to explain that.

00:27:43.090 --> 00:27:47.350
But Matt would say that
need your sleep, period.

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:50.050
I'm more of the camp, based
on my read of the data,

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:53.470
And, yes, we are allowed to
disagree and still be friends.

00:27:53.470 --> 00:27:56.280
It's allowed.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:58.670
In fact, Matt's going
to do a series on sleep

00:27:58.670 --> 00:28:00.170
with our podcast,
even though he has

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:03.320
a terrific podcast of his
own, where we will maybe

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:06.380
debate a little bit of this,
that there are ways that you

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:09.715
can at least replace the
feeling of wakefulness

00:28:09.715 --> 00:28:11.840
that you would have lost
if you don't sleep enough.

00:28:11.840 --> 00:28:13.790
And for me, really,
that's why NSDR

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:18.440
became such an attractive tool
to do for 10 or 30 minutes each

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:20.960
morning if I didn't sleep
enough the night before.

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:22.460
I first learned
about yoga nidra,

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:25.790
actually at addiction recovery
center, trauma recovery center

00:28:25.790 --> 00:28:27.710
in Florida in 2017.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:31.070
I have a friend, a super
talented trauma therapist,

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:35.480
who also treats addiction, that
I've sent many, many people to,

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:39.920
and he has this kind of
seemingly like wizard ability

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:43.610
to get people who have been
addicts to not be addicts.

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:50.090
And one of the tools he uses
is yoga nidra every morning

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:52.230
for 30 minutes, and
eventually an hour,

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:53.840
which seems like
a lot, but then he

00:28:53.840 --> 00:28:57.420
also has these people wake
up very early, maybe an hour

00:28:57.420 --> 00:29:00.210
before they would
normally wake up, and go

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.880
into that liminal state
between sleep and wakefulness.

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:06.960
Now, my experience is that 10 to
20 minutes of NSDR, yoga nidra,

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:11.400
is sufficient to
offset some sleep loss

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:12.900
and allow at least
me to function,

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:14.340
and many people report the same.

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:17.580
We have a study going with the
Sleep Laboratory at Stanford

00:29:17.580 --> 00:29:18.870
to explore this in more depth.

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:20.310
And what I can tell
you, because I'm

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:21.685
involved in some
of this work, is

00:29:21.685 --> 00:29:25.260
that there are several military
units, because they have

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.230
no opportunity to get sleep
because they're working,

00:29:28.230 --> 00:29:30.240
that have to rely
on tools like this

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:33.750
in order to be able to function
at their highest level.

00:29:33.750 --> 00:29:35.970
And I'm sure they will
tell you, as I will,

00:29:35.970 --> 00:29:38.970
that they'd prefer to get
8 to 10 hours of sleep.

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:40.680
But, guess what,
they can't, and so I

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:43.080
think that's the important
takeaway is that we don't get

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:44.970
to pick how much we
sleep, unless you're

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:48.240
going to be completely neurotic
about your sleep hygiene, which

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:50.490
makes you kind of a less
interesting person in life,

00:29:50.490 --> 00:29:52.200
is what I'm told.

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:56.220
Going to bed at 8:00 is great
like some or most of the time

00:29:56.220 --> 00:29:58.320
but you got to stay up
every once in a while.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:02.010
I mean, after they released
"Chimp Empire" on Netflix,

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:05.790
I discovered that NSDR
is a very valuable tool.

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:08.160
And by the way, "Chimp
Empire" and "Succession"

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:09.780
have a lot of parallels.

00:30:09.780 --> 00:30:13.088
And if you watch one, I
interleave "Chimp Empire",

00:30:13.088 --> 00:30:14.880
"Succession", "Chimp
Empire", "Succession".

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:19.360
And you start to realize, like,
whoa, like were pretty similar.

00:30:19.360 --> 00:30:22.740
And then you look at the
world differently, I promise.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:24.780
Your podcast has
positively changed

00:30:24.780 --> 00:30:27.030
the lives of so many
people, including me,

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.880
how has it changed your life?

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:31.400
OK.

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:32.940
I wasn't expecting that one.

00:30:32.940 --> 00:30:34.500
Thank you, Samantha.

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:38.620
Well, first of all, I mean,
as this little 11-year-old

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:39.120
told me.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:43.540
I mean, this is essentially
what I've done my whole life.

00:30:43.540 --> 00:30:45.120
I'm a fairly private person.

00:30:45.120 --> 00:30:47.310
Believe it or not, I'm
pretty introverted.

00:30:47.310 --> 00:30:53.040
I spend a lot of time alone, and
I think that's required for me.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:55.480
I basically have four modes.

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:56.230
Four modes.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:31:03.100
One, I'm either readying
myself through sleep and NSDR

00:31:03.100 --> 00:31:05.950
to do one of the
other three modes,

00:31:05.950 --> 00:31:08.710
maybe there's a fifth mode, or
I'm in one of these other three

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:11.170
modes, which is I'm either
foraging for information,

00:31:11.170 --> 00:31:14.480
organizing that information,
or dispersing that information,

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:17.430
or getting ready to
do it all over again.

00:31:17.430 --> 00:31:19.430
And then, there's this
relaxation vacation thing

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:20.690
that they keep telling me about.

00:31:20.690 --> 00:31:22.580
But then I went to
Italy, and then like Rick

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:27.890
and I like just hung out there,
and it didn't feel like work.

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:29.870
I also discovered some
really great podcasts.

00:31:29.870 --> 00:31:32.240
I don't know, I think
one of the coolest

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.780
podcasts out there if you like
rock and roll, which I love,

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:39.840
is A History of 500 Songs by
Andrew Hickey's podcast on rock

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:40.340
and roll.

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:41.423
I like the nerdy podcasts.

00:31:41.423 --> 00:31:44.150
It's like a graduate or an
education in rock and roll.

00:31:44.150 --> 00:31:46.670
It's so cool.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:48.950
And you'll learn a lot
about music and history

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:50.752
and the mobs involved
and all that stuff.

00:31:50.752 --> 00:31:53.210
And from what I was told, like
Al Capone used to sit there,

00:31:53.210 --> 00:31:53.710
right?

00:31:53.710 --> 00:31:56.840
His exit was there, you
know, so it was weird.

00:31:56.840 --> 00:31:57.890
Right?

00:31:57.890 --> 00:31:59.600
And then he died of
syphilis, and like

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:02.090
so I don't know how I
feel about all that,

00:32:02.090 --> 00:32:05.360
but they told me that.

00:32:05.360 --> 00:32:11.000
So I think the podcast has been
wonderful as an opportunity

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:13.880
to share things that I love.

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:15.900
If I had my way, it
would be more like this,

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:18.280
although more of a
dialogue, frankly.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:25.630
It's changed my understanding
of what the world is like.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:30.090
I certainly get critique,
and that's good.

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:32.220
But, again, I was
raised by iconoclasts,

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:34.860
and particularly my postdoc
advisor, Ben Barrus,

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:36.460
who unfortunately,
as I mentioned,

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:40.110
is dead because
I worked for him.

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:44.580
But he really encouraged
all of us in his lab.

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.780
And often, we were
very close friends.

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:48.648
I spent the last year
of his life recording

00:32:48.648 --> 00:32:49.440
interview with him.

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:52.590
There's actually a documentary
coming out about Ben,

00:32:52.590 --> 00:32:56.160
and then I'm going to release
the audio interviews with Ben,

00:32:56.160 --> 00:32:57.570
which he approved, by the way.

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:01.230
And you'll get to realize that
the history of what you see

00:33:01.230 --> 00:33:04.428
is often not what the dead
person really did or said.

00:33:04.428 --> 00:33:05.970
It's going to be
fun, so I can't wait

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:07.428
till they release
this documentary,

00:33:07.428 --> 00:33:10.127
and then Ben gets to have
his voice infused in it.

00:33:10.127 --> 00:33:11.460
And it'll be a cool documentary.

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:15.870
But, as some of you know, that
what appears to be and what was

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.870
and the historical
narrative is not necessarily

00:33:18.870 --> 00:33:21.490
what really happened,
but that's just life.

00:33:21.490 --> 00:33:25.110
So I suppose the short answer
is that I feel very honored

00:33:25.110 --> 00:33:26.340
with the opportunity.

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:28.350
Very, very honored.

00:33:28.350 --> 00:33:32.340
And it's a challenge at times.

00:33:32.340 --> 00:33:33.780
I suppose the major
challenges are

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:36.890
when things are taken out of
context, like little clips,

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:37.890
and things of that sort.

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.900
Being misunderstood
doesn't feel good.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:42.690
But look, at the
end of the day, I

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:45.900
feel like the luckiest
person in the world

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:48.520
because I get to spend
my time learning.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:51.330
So it's either I'm
foraging, I'm organizing

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.330
or I'm dispersing
information that I also want

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:57.990
and that I find
incredibly useful.

00:33:57.990 --> 00:34:01.590
Or if not that, then certainly
informative and, at times,

00:34:01.590 --> 00:34:03.190
enchanting as well.

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:05.110
So I try and focus
on the positive,

00:34:05.110 --> 00:34:08.800
and I have a number of
practices that help me do that.

00:34:08.800 --> 00:34:11.170
And I am somebody who
engages on social media.

00:34:11.170 --> 00:34:12.989
I'm not a post and
ghost kind of person.

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:15.130
I want to understand.

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:17.270
I think that cuttlefish
are super interesting,

00:34:17.270 --> 00:34:19.340
but human beings are
super interesting too.

00:34:19.340 --> 00:34:22.969
And I have a strong
drive for mastery,

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:27.350
but also I do have a
strong sense of justice,

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:29.587
and that can be
problematic at times.

00:34:29.587 --> 00:34:31.670
I define justice as feeling
like there's something

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:34.040
for us to do about something.

00:34:34.040 --> 00:34:35.610
Like seeing something
that upsets us

00:34:35.610 --> 00:34:37.257
or that excites us
is great, but then

00:34:37.257 --> 00:34:38.840
if you have a strong
sense of justice,

00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:41.173
you feel like there's something
you need to do about it,

00:34:41.173 --> 00:34:42.420
and not everybody feels that.

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:44.570
And then, of course, a
desire to understand.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.449
And I think, for reasons
very personal to me,

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:49.580
that relate mostly
to just having

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:52.770
a kind of incredible array
of experiences in life,

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:56.330
many of which were like
shocking, disturbing, exciting,

00:34:56.330 --> 00:34:58.440
enchanting that I
want to understand.

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:04.020
And so it's changed things,
but it's like, I don't know,

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.260
I got one truck, one watch.

00:35:06.260 --> 00:35:10.268
Which is not to say that I
don't care about having things.

00:35:10.268 --> 00:35:11.810
There are a few
things I really love,

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:14.970
but mostly, like I'm just
thinking about the podcast

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.920
we got to record
on Monday which is

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:18.315
about willpower and tenacity.

00:35:18.315 --> 00:35:20.440
And if I don't stop myself,
I'll give it right now.

00:35:20.440 --> 00:35:23.220
So what do you feel
is the next big thing

00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:25.313
to come to the forefront
in the health space?

00:35:25.313 --> 00:35:26.355
Thanks for this question.

00:35:29.080 --> 00:35:32.430
One is non-protein amino acids.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.190
I love these debates
online because I know

00:35:35.190 --> 00:35:36.600
that there's something there.

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:39.580
It's so cool because I grew
up in seeing these debates,

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:41.580
and you know there's
something interesting there

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:43.380
because people are
debating about it.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:45.433
But the debate isn't
what's interesting.

00:35:45.433 --> 00:35:47.100
It's almost always
the thing that people

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:50.470
aren't talking about that's
sure to surface at some point.

00:35:50.470 --> 00:35:51.690
And right now, there's.

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:54.510
This obsession with like
seed oils it's like seed oil.

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:55.050
Seed oils.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.610
And I can't demonize seed oils.

00:35:56.610 --> 00:35:58.710
All the data say that
if you separate out

00:35:58.710 --> 00:36:01.170
their caloric load, there's
nothing inherently bad

00:36:01.170 --> 00:36:02.760
about seed oils, and et cetera.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:03.480
I don't know.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:04.897
It's still an
emerging literature.

00:36:04.897 --> 00:36:10.890
But there was a scientist at
Stanford, Ed Rubenstein, who

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:15.600
passed away at a ripe old age,
a brilliant scientist, who

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:17.610
talked about, and I had
a lot of discussions

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:21.250
with when I was a postdoc,
about non-protein amino acids.

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:23.910
It turns out his son is
a neuroscientist at UCSF.

00:36:23.910 --> 00:36:26.580
His other son is a
physician at UCSF.

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:30.370
So it's another one of these
low performing families.

00:36:30.370 --> 00:36:33.310
And all wonderful people.

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:41.270
And you know, Ed had data
that unlike a lot of animals,

00:36:41.270 --> 00:36:44.750
like birds, there are
certain amino acids, which

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:46.940
are non-protein
amino acids, that

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:50.150
exist in fairly high
density in seeds and nuts,

00:36:50.150 --> 00:36:52.760
which is not to say that
seeds and nuts are bad.

00:36:52.760 --> 00:36:56.900
I had almonds this
evening, so please.

00:36:56.900 --> 00:37:01.100
But non-protein amino
acids are similar enough

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:08.380
to mammalian protein amino
acids that if they are consumed

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:11.350
in abundance, and
perhaps especially

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:14.085
in liquid form, that
they may, again, may,

00:37:14.085 --> 00:37:15.460
because this is
about the future.

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:16.390
This isn't about what we know.

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:17.848
This is about where
I think there's

00:37:17.848 --> 00:37:19.330
interest and growing interest.

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:22.570
They may be able to incorporate
into certain proteins

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:25.960
of our tissues of our
body that potentially Ed

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.270
thought could lead to
misfolding of those proteins

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.940
and may explain certain
forms of neurodegeneration

00:37:30.940 --> 00:37:34.190
and other neurocognitive
challenges.

00:37:34.190 --> 00:37:36.220
So I think non-protein
amino acids are going

00:37:36.220 --> 00:37:37.840
to be an important discussion.

00:37:37.840 --> 00:37:41.440
Now, there are lots of
non protein amino acids

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:43.057
that don't come
from foods, but I

00:37:43.057 --> 00:37:45.640
think the discussion around non
protein amino acids from foods

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:48.380
is going to be very interesting.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:49.867
So that's one area.

00:37:49.867 --> 00:37:52.450
The other area, and perhaps you
picked up on this a little bit

00:37:52.450 --> 00:37:55.690
tonight, is that I like the nuts
and boltsy stuff around stress,

00:37:55.690 --> 00:37:56.920
neuroplasticity.

00:37:56.920 --> 00:38:02.450
But the high level stuff,
I think the relationship

00:38:02.450 --> 00:38:08.030
between structured
thought, abstract thought,

00:38:08.030 --> 00:38:10.530
I'm not interested in
the free will discussion.

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:13.220
I just feel like
that's a career ender.

00:38:13.220 --> 00:38:14.630
I'm friends with
Robert Sapolsky,

00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:16.130
and he's got a great
book coming out

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:18.110
about this called
"Determined", so he obviously

00:38:18.110 --> 00:38:19.318
doesn't believe in free will.

00:38:19.318 --> 00:38:21.380
But he's far smarter
than I ever could be.

00:38:21.380 --> 00:38:25.160
And it just feels like,
I don't know that there's

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:26.930
an endpoint with that one.

00:38:26.930 --> 00:38:30.200
But if anyone could
find, it would be Robert.

00:38:30.200 --> 00:38:32.070
He's oh so smart.

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:39.130
But I think that the higher
level stuff, creativity,

00:38:39.130 --> 00:38:42.040
abstract reasoning and
thought, defining and better

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:43.510
understanding the
different states

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:45.070
that we can go into in waking.

00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:50.260
And I confess, without a hint
of sheepishness about it,

00:38:50.260 --> 00:38:53.800
that I also think the
notion of spirituality

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:56.620
and the belief in
things that are

00:38:56.620 --> 00:39:00.490
beyond our current and
conscious understanding

00:39:00.490 --> 00:39:02.630
is super interesting.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:09.280
I think that as a species we've
been challenged and conflicted

00:39:09.280 --> 00:39:13.240
from go, at least that's what
the historical scripture tells

00:39:13.240 --> 00:39:16.630
us, and that it's sort of
inherent to our experience

00:39:16.630 --> 00:39:21.370
that life is challenging and
perplexing and also wonderful.

00:39:21.370 --> 00:39:23.260
And so I think a
better understanding

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:27.635
of how to navigate all that, I
mean, this stuff in our skulls,

00:39:27.635 --> 00:39:29.260
except for the eyes,
which, by the way,

00:39:29.260 --> 00:39:31.030
are your two pieces
of brain that are not

00:39:31.030 --> 00:39:32.665
in your skull, the retinas.

00:39:32.665 --> 00:39:35.930
I have to point that out.

00:39:35.930 --> 00:39:39.490
You know, it's tricky, and we're
trying to use that very tissue

00:39:39.490 --> 00:39:41.000
to understand it.

00:39:41.000 --> 00:39:44.470
And so I'm intrigued
by the possibility

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:51.070
that there are certain aspects
of self that maybe are not

00:39:51.070 --> 00:39:54.490
intended to be explored
because they are not really

00:39:54.490 --> 00:39:55.840
of us, right?

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:57.765
Brains interacting
with one another

00:39:57.765 --> 00:39:59.140
is an interesting
area, certainly

00:39:59.140 --> 00:40:01.210
for scientific
exploration, but I'm

00:40:01.210 --> 00:40:04.030
fascinated by and excited
about the possibility

00:40:04.030 --> 00:40:08.060
that, at some point, our
species will both understand

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:11.200
and the mechanics
of our emotions,

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:14.570
mechanics of our thought
process, creativity, and so on,

00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:17.470
but that we will also
allow room for the stuff

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:21.670
that we can't explain with
science and to allow room

00:40:21.670 --> 00:40:25.240
for that in our life
experience because I also

00:40:25.240 --> 00:40:26.720
believe that can be powerful.

00:40:26.720 --> 00:40:30.310
And while understanding
things in great detail

00:40:30.310 --> 00:40:33.250
and putting mechanism
and utility around that

00:40:33.250 --> 00:40:36.430
and applying that
is wonderful, it's

00:40:36.430 --> 00:40:40.750
the stuff of my life,
which is obvious,

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:43.930
I also think that there's
great value in not

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:46.030
trying to control and
understand everything

00:40:46.030 --> 00:40:49.270
and enjoying the
mystery of things that

00:40:49.270 --> 00:40:51.205
are clearly greater than us.

00:40:53.935 --> 00:40:57.400
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:03.250
How do you balance
having fun with having

00:41:03.250 --> 00:41:05.800
such a rich knowledge and
passion in neuroscience

00:41:05.800 --> 00:41:07.240
and optimization?

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:09.738
Fun.

00:41:09.738 --> 00:41:11.530
People ask me, like
what do you do for fun?

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:14.050
I think people think I
do all the protocols.

00:41:14.050 --> 00:41:17.270
It's like, sure, I get my
sunlight, I drink my water.

00:41:17.270 --> 00:41:17.770
I do.

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:18.880
I do them.

00:41:18.880 --> 00:41:27.000
But fun, I like learning.

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:28.850
I like learning.

00:41:28.850 --> 00:41:30.560
I do enjoy physical movement.

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:31.490
I like learning.

00:41:31.490 --> 00:41:35.480
For me, OK, so like for
me, it's the little things.

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:38.300
I don't know, maybe I'm weird.

00:41:38.300 --> 00:41:40.760
Certainly, I've been told that.

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:43.860
The other day, there was a
frog in my swimming pool,

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:46.130
and he was just like
sitting there like.

00:41:46.130 --> 00:41:50.690
And I just like spent some time
looking at him back and forth,

00:41:50.690 --> 00:41:53.302
and then I had all sorts of
ideas about what's he doing?

00:41:53.302 --> 00:41:54.260
And what's he thinking?

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:57.080
And then I was thinking
how Oliver Sacks used

00:41:57.080 --> 00:42:02.240
to spend time imagining
experiencing life as a bat

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:03.650
through only echolocation.

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:05.000
He talked about this.

00:42:05.000 --> 00:42:07.430
And I thought, and
why would he do that?

00:42:07.430 --> 00:42:07.970
Right?

00:42:07.970 --> 00:42:09.030
And that's kind of cute.

00:42:09.030 --> 00:42:11.090
He's like delightful
old man, right?

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:13.970
He had a 600 pounds free weight
squat, won the state California

00:42:13.970 --> 00:42:16.560
squatting championship but also
liked pretending he was a bat.

00:42:16.560 --> 00:42:17.330
He's a weird dude.

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:20.060
Did methamphetamines,
raced motorcycles,

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:23.490
hung out with movie stars, but
then also was like a recluse

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:24.480
and liked rocks.

00:42:24.480 --> 00:42:27.210
OK, that's a weird scientist.

00:42:27.210 --> 00:42:30.485
Been around a lot of those.

00:42:30.485 --> 00:42:32.360
But in discussions with
people that knew him,

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:35.090
I mean, that exercise of like
seeing something he thought

00:42:35.090 --> 00:42:40.243
was cool like a bat allowed
him to also use theory of mind

00:42:40.243 --> 00:42:41.660
to kind of think,
what would it be

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:43.370
like to have locked
in syndrome and only

00:42:43.370 --> 00:42:46.130
be able to blink your
eyes to communicate?

00:42:46.130 --> 00:42:49.100
And gave him an incredible
compassion and sensitivity

00:42:49.100 --> 00:42:53.180
for other people that
then he transmuted

00:42:53.180 --> 00:42:55.500
into the form of
these wonderful books.

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:58.790
So for me, fun is really
about doing the things

00:42:58.790 --> 00:43:00.530
that I do
systematically each day,

00:43:00.530 --> 00:43:02.840
but then when
something excites me.

00:43:02.840 --> 00:43:04.350
I know that feeling.

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:06.380
I know it physically in my body.

00:43:06.380 --> 00:43:10.190
And to just follow that
trail like a weirdo, right?

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:12.440
Like a weirdo, just
like Barrus was weird

00:43:12.440 --> 00:43:14.510
and Barbara Chapman was weird.

00:43:14.510 --> 00:43:16.610
And like, I think
everybody's a little weird

00:43:16.610 --> 00:43:18.980
if you allow yourself to
just see the things that you

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:20.130
think are really cool.

00:43:20.130 --> 00:43:21.110
And there are a bunch
of things that I

00:43:21.110 --> 00:43:23.000
think are really
cool that enchant me

00:43:23.000 --> 00:43:25.230
that a bunch of other
people love too.

00:43:25.230 --> 00:43:29.030
And I know that because I see
we're all watching that video

00:43:29.030 --> 00:43:31.110
or something like that.

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:34.400
But I think for me,
fun is in the practice

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:39.430
of trying to stay open to
like the little things.

00:43:39.430 --> 00:43:41.180
The little things that
kids say are always

00:43:41.180 --> 00:43:42.847
delightful because
they're not filtering

00:43:42.847 --> 00:43:45.200
through all the bullshit
that we filter our life

00:43:45.200 --> 00:43:46.130
experience through.

00:43:46.130 --> 00:43:49.370
But also just, yeah, I
like reading and learning,

00:43:49.370 --> 00:43:50.330
exercising.

00:43:50.330 --> 00:43:54.020
I mean, I like to think I'm
not a very wooden person.

00:43:54.020 --> 00:44:00.560
But, at the same time, I mean,
I didn't see the "Barbie" movie.

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:03.026
No disrespect to Barbie.

00:44:03.026 --> 00:44:04.310
I go to movies.

00:44:04.310 --> 00:44:05.310
I do that kind of thing.

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:07.490
I don't know what
else is there to do?

00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:12.380
At some point, you know,
what else is there to do?

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:15.350
Anyway, I mean, I delight
in certain things,

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:17.240
as you now know
far too much about.

00:44:17.240 --> 00:44:22.160
But fun is a relative term.

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:23.630
That was a hard question.

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:27.530
[AUDIENCE LAUGHING &amp; APPLAUDING]

00:44:27.530 --> 00:44:28.460
Thank you.

00:44:28.460 --> 00:44:30.010
Thanks for the pass.

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:31.000
Thanks for that.

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:33.490
Do you believe time changes
due to daylight savings

00:44:33.490 --> 00:44:36.190
time is worth a potential
loss of productivity?

00:44:36.190 --> 00:44:38.380
Daylight saving
time is anti-health.

00:44:38.380 --> 00:44:41.560
OK, this is where I'll
get like, it's just dumb.

00:44:41.560 --> 00:44:43.150
It's just dumb.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:44.560
It's just so dumb.

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:45.890
It makes no sense.

00:44:45.890 --> 00:44:48.580
I mean, the director of
the Chronobiology Unit

00:44:48.580 --> 00:44:51.460
at the National Institutes of
Mental Health, my good friend,

00:44:51.460 --> 00:44:54.190
long time friend and brilliant
scientist, Samer Hattar,

00:44:54.190 --> 00:44:56.200
will tell you,
it's a stupid idea.

00:44:56.200 --> 00:44:57.940
It's anti-biology.

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:00.790
And increased car crashes,
increase heart attacks,

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:03.070
increased depression.

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:04.930
Kids don't like to
wake up early anyway.

00:45:04.930 --> 00:45:05.980
Parents don't like
to wake up early,

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:08.105
especially with kids that
don't like wake up early.

00:45:08.105 --> 00:45:09.200
It makes no sense.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:10.783
And then there are
all these arguments

00:45:10.783 --> 00:45:15.520
about is really about
trying to truncate the late,

00:45:15.520 --> 00:45:18.250
you want more light in the
evening so there's less crime.

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:20.025
Like, that's totally
unsubstantiated.

00:45:20.025 --> 00:45:21.400
Like, completely
unsubstantiated.

00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:24.070
So the daylight savings
thing is just stupid.

00:45:24.070 --> 00:45:26.320
Basically, try and get as
much light in your eyes,

00:45:26.320 --> 00:45:28.560
ideally from sunlight
early in the day.

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:31.040
And, by the way, if you're
worried about cataract,

00:45:31.040 --> 00:45:32.090
that's a serious concern.

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:34.173
After all, I have an
appointment in ophthalmology.

00:45:34.173 --> 00:45:36.050
Cataract, macular degeneration.

00:45:36.050 --> 00:45:39.620
But guess what, the chair of
ophthalmology from Stanford

00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:41.540
when he came on the
podcast verified this.

00:45:41.540 --> 00:45:43.760
When the sun is low
in the sky, you're

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:45.537
not really at risk of that.

00:45:45.537 --> 00:45:47.120
So when the sun is
overhead and you're

00:45:47.120 --> 00:45:49.100
like beaming in your
eyes, like trying to get,

00:45:49.100 --> 00:45:51.140
yeah, it's a problem,
but we're talking

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:53.648
about viewing low solar
angle sunlight in the morning

00:45:53.648 --> 00:45:54.440
and in the evening.

00:45:54.440 --> 00:45:56.030
And if there's
clouds, do it anyway.

00:45:56.030 --> 00:45:56.970
In fact, do it longer.

00:45:56.970 --> 00:45:59.600
And if you can't do that,
look at some artificial light

00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:00.440
inside.

00:46:00.440 --> 00:46:04.760
Daylight savings,
it's just stupid.

00:46:04.760 --> 00:46:08.047
You know, what happens here
is as the night goes on,

00:46:08.047 --> 00:46:10.130
the amount of GABA in my
brain starts to diminish,

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:11.660
and I just kind of
go to short form.

00:46:11.660 --> 00:46:13.520
We've thought about podcasting
in the middle of the night.

00:46:13.520 --> 00:46:16.340
That's why when I went on Lex's
podcast recently, the more

00:46:16.340 --> 00:46:19.880
recent one, he did it at 8:00
o'clock at night, and he cried.

00:46:19.880 --> 00:46:20.827
He made me cry.

00:46:20.827 --> 00:46:21.410
He didn't cry.

00:46:21.410 --> 00:46:22.790
He made me cry.

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:24.493
I was so tired,
and I can't think,

00:46:24.493 --> 00:46:25.910
and then he asked
me about my dog,

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:27.710
and I'm talking about the dog.

00:46:27.710 --> 00:46:29.600
So his goal was
to get me to cry.

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:31.910
We have this
friendship, you know.

00:46:31.910 --> 00:46:33.800
No, he's delightful.

00:46:33.800 --> 00:46:36.230
What should I as a
19-year-old college student

00:46:36.230 --> 00:46:39.290
be doing to maximize the years
of neuroplasticity I have left?

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:41.090
I get this question,
I'm like, oh, man.

00:46:41.090 --> 00:46:42.470
It's such a great question.

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:45.182
Rhys, I'm assuming, yeah.

00:46:45.182 --> 00:46:47.390
I don't know who you are,
Rhys, or what you're doing,

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:48.620
but you're 19.

00:46:48.620 --> 00:46:51.110
So the cool thing is,
your brain is hypoplastic.

00:46:51.110 --> 00:46:56.310
Life is a psychedelic
experience without psychedelics.

00:46:56.310 --> 00:46:58.320
Gosh, people always
say, like, if you

00:46:58.320 --> 00:47:00.150
could go back to your
19-year-old self,

00:47:00.150 --> 00:47:00.900
what would you do?

00:47:00.900 --> 00:47:01.950
And that's a tricky one.

00:47:01.950 --> 00:47:03.408
There are movies
about that, right?

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:10.140
You know, I would
definitely worry less.

00:47:10.140 --> 00:47:10.680
Yeah.

00:47:10.680 --> 00:47:11.490
I would worry less.

00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:14.790
I would have more fun.

00:47:14.790 --> 00:47:18.750
I would certainly,
listen, I started

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:21.720
latching on to practices and
the understanding of science

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:24.300
as a way, for me it was
kind of like my world

00:47:24.300 --> 00:47:26.970
felt very unstable.

00:47:26.970 --> 00:47:29.160
And for me, it
brought stability.

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:30.810
But you want to avoid rigidity.

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:34.950
So do you want to be the one
19-year-old who's like, oh,

00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:36.210
got to get to sleep.

00:47:36.210 --> 00:47:39.870
You know, I got to go back
to bed at 8:30 at night.

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:40.960
Enjoy life.

00:47:40.960 --> 00:47:47.090
But I would say, when you're
19, learn how you learn.

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:48.400
Learn how to focus.

00:47:48.400 --> 00:47:49.570
Learn how to rest.

00:47:49.570 --> 00:47:51.440
Basically, you can
stress and focus

00:47:51.440 --> 00:47:53.773
as much as you want as long
as you can still fall asleep

00:47:53.773 --> 00:47:56.890
at night and sleep well and
fall back asleep if you wake up.

00:47:56.890 --> 00:47:59.235
We hear stress is bad, stress
is good, stress is bad,

00:47:59.235 --> 00:47:59.860
stress is good.

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:02.260
Stress is bad unless
you're getting

00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:03.970
enough sleep, in
which case, stress is

00:48:03.970 --> 00:48:05.050
called learning in life.

00:48:05.050 --> 00:48:06.883
Now, obviously, don't
do anything dangerous.

00:48:06.883 --> 00:48:09.400
Avoid psychological
and physical danger.

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:15.110
But I think as a 19-year-old,
I mean my direct advice would

00:48:15.110 --> 00:48:17.780
have some cardiovascular
activity you like,

00:48:17.780 --> 00:48:21.050
have some resistance training
activity you like, develop

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:23.495
some sort of self awareness
practice like journaling.

00:48:23.495 --> 00:48:26.690
It could be meditation.

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:29.570
Surround yourself with
people that you like.

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:31.670
Avoid people you don't like.

00:48:31.670 --> 00:48:33.590
I mean, it sounds so
straightforward but then

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:35.030
we can all look into
our personal histories

00:48:35.030 --> 00:48:36.620
and be like, wow, I spent
all this time trying

00:48:36.620 --> 00:48:39.037
to resolve this thing that,
clearly, you didn't like them.

00:48:39.037 --> 00:48:40.010
It's simple.

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:40.890
You didn't like them.

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:42.557
They weren't into
cuttlefish or ferrets.

00:48:42.557 --> 00:48:43.432
You didn't like them.

00:48:43.432 --> 00:48:44.900
Wasn't your kind
of person, right?

00:48:44.900 --> 00:48:47.240
And that's not a box you can
check on the dating apps,

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:49.010
they tell me, right?

00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:51.710
The ferret thing, I think
it'd be very, very, very

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:53.810
small subset of people.

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:57.360
I think the know
thyself thing is huge.

00:48:57.360 --> 00:49:01.310
Learn to tap into that
early feeling of like this

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:01.820
feels right.

00:49:01.820 --> 00:49:02.820
This doesn't feel right.

00:49:02.820 --> 00:49:05.720
Learn to be a bit of
a rudder for yourself.

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:07.760
And journal.

00:49:07.760 --> 00:49:09.650
I still have stacks
of things that I

00:49:09.650 --> 00:49:12.320
wrote across the years,
most of it is terrible,

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:15.570
but you will find if you go
back that you kind of knew

00:49:15.570 --> 00:49:20.100
better all along even if you
didn't do better all along.

00:49:20.100 --> 00:49:23.670
That voice in your
head, don't do that.

00:49:23.670 --> 00:49:24.810
Do that.

00:49:24.810 --> 00:49:26.400
This person's like
everyone else is

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:29.963
crazy about this
person but not me.

00:49:29.963 --> 00:49:31.380
I don't want to
sound sentimental,

00:49:31.380 --> 00:49:36.605
but you have to find your heart,
right, or at least not lose it.

00:49:36.605 --> 00:49:37.980
You have to make
sure that you're

00:49:37.980 --> 00:49:40.650
in touch with that piece
of yourself that wasn't

00:49:40.650 --> 00:49:42.515
judging and just felt good.

00:49:42.515 --> 00:49:43.890
And as long as
it's not something

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:45.900
that's self-destructive, I
think that's the most important

00:49:45.900 --> 00:49:46.210
thing.

00:49:46.210 --> 00:49:48.450
And then, yeah, have tools
and practices in place,

00:49:48.450 --> 00:49:50.250
because they work,
and when you're 19,

00:49:50.250 --> 00:49:53.070
and your neural neuroplasticity
is through the roof,

00:49:53.070 --> 00:49:56.160
you can do a lot less
and get a lot more.

00:49:56.160 --> 00:49:59.790
But don't worry about hitting
25 and it all being over.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:01.440
Certainly, that's not the case.

00:50:01.440 --> 00:50:04.970
But you can cram
in a lot early on.

00:50:04.970 --> 00:50:06.810
I still regret not
learning an instrument.

00:50:06.810 --> 00:50:10.560
The data on people that learn an
instrument even later in life,

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:13.470
but certainly at 19, is that it
greatly increases your ability

00:50:13.470 --> 00:50:15.340
to learn all sorts of things.

00:50:15.340 --> 00:50:16.830
So learn an instrument.

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:19.260
How can we transform the
American education system

00:50:19.260 --> 00:50:20.680
to be more effective?

00:50:20.680 --> 00:50:23.670
Oh, so in ten seconds.

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:27.018
[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:29.650
So when I'm in charge.

00:50:29.650 --> 00:50:30.610
Nah, I'm just kidding.

00:50:30.610 --> 00:50:32.920
A little while back,
I did an interview

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:35.740
with a major media outlet,
and they were very gracious.

00:50:35.740 --> 00:50:37.430
And then they said,
like, what's next?

00:50:37.430 --> 00:50:39.263
And I was like, I'm
going to run for office.

00:50:39.263 --> 00:50:43.300
I was just like, look, it's
not out of the question.

00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:46.450
But frankly, I think that I'm
so poorly suited for that,

00:50:46.450 --> 00:50:48.190
it's obvious.

00:50:48.190 --> 00:50:49.820
I'm so poorly suited for that.

00:50:49.820 --> 00:50:52.955
So it wasn't a joke, but I think
as I spent some time afterwards

00:50:52.955 --> 00:50:54.580
like, I should really
go talk to people

00:50:54.580 --> 00:50:57.580
who do that for a living.

00:50:57.580 --> 00:51:02.860
But I am very interested in
potentially informing policy.

00:51:02.860 --> 00:51:06.040
If I were to ever be asked,
I'd certainly respond.

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:10.895
And my stance on this is much
in the same vein as the podcast,

00:51:10.895 --> 00:51:12.520
because keep in mind,
the podcast, yes,

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:17.770
like suggested I start one, but
it was really during those days

00:51:17.770 --> 00:51:22.930
of the deep 2020 and 2021
pandemic where, by the way,

00:51:22.930 --> 00:51:25.330
I wasn't allowed to talk
about vaccines so I didn't.

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:27.300
And also, I don't have
any expertise in it,

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:29.880
so I was not well
suited to do it.

00:51:29.880 --> 00:51:32.300
But I also decided it's a
topic that enough people are

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:32.970
talking about.

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:35.160
So my response in
life, and in general,

00:51:35.160 --> 00:51:37.527
when people say, what
about something is I like,

00:51:37.527 --> 00:51:39.360
well, I've got a lot
of opinions about that,

00:51:39.360 --> 00:51:40.440
but this isn't the venue.

00:51:40.440 --> 00:51:40.940
OK.

00:51:40.940 --> 00:51:44.810
But during the
pandemic, I realized

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:48.432
there was a lot of circadian
disruption, anxiety, stress,

00:51:48.432 --> 00:51:50.390
a bunch of things happening
with visual systems

00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:52.400
and biological
systems that I felt

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:55.422
there were tools that people
could, perhaps, benefit from.

00:51:55.422 --> 00:51:57.630
So I started putting that
information into the world,

00:51:57.630 --> 00:52:00.290
and I was really
surprised, really

00:52:00.290 --> 00:52:02.255
surprised that
people that I knew

00:52:02.255 --> 00:52:04.130
from the neuroscience
community, for instance

00:52:04.130 --> 00:52:08.360
at NIMH or in
government positions,

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:12.870
weren't talking about this
stuff, so I just kept doing it.

00:52:12.870 --> 00:52:17.840
And I think that
it made clear to me

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:21.837
that the education
system is not one thing.

00:52:21.837 --> 00:52:24.170
It's tough, especially when
talking about kids like what

00:52:24.170 --> 00:52:27.650
to do is a tricky thing.

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:30.720
But that the education
system, in my mind,

00:52:30.720 --> 00:52:34.040
should at least involve
some sort of discussion

00:52:34.040 --> 00:52:36.980
early on about this
thing called the brain,

00:52:36.980 --> 00:52:38.960
this thing called
the body, how they

00:52:38.960 --> 00:52:42.860
work, how you can do certain
things to modulate your stress

00:52:42.860 --> 00:52:46.370
level, your sleep not just
the importance of sleep

00:52:46.370 --> 00:52:49.640
and nutrition and avoiding
social isolation and all

00:52:49.640 --> 00:52:52.130
these important things, but
giving people some levers

00:52:52.130 --> 00:52:58.220
and knobs to maneuver within
themselves and zero cost tools

00:52:58.220 --> 00:53:01.820
as a way to do
that that transcend

00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:04.737
socioeconomic boundaries,
ideally transcend language

00:53:04.737 --> 00:53:07.070
as well, because we're not
just talking about the United

00:53:07.070 --> 00:53:07.400
States.

00:53:07.400 --> 00:53:08.750
And of course, within
the United States,

00:53:08.750 --> 00:53:10.340
there are many
different cultures

00:53:10.340 --> 00:53:11.990
speaking different languages.

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:14.210
And maybe AI will soon
allow us to put out

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:16.300
the podcast into a bunch
of different languages.

00:53:16.300 --> 00:53:17.890
I think it will.

00:53:17.890 --> 00:53:23.340
So I think the education system
should start, in my opinion,

00:53:23.340 --> 00:53:26.700
with teaching kids how
to understand themselves,

00:53:26.700 --> 00:53:28.890
what to do in difficult
scenarios that's really

00:53:28.890 --> 00:53:33.300
anchored in the real pillars
of biology and psychology,

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:35.970
and take some of the
mystery out of trying

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:39.750
to navigate the tough
business of growing up.

00:53:39.750 --> 00:53:42.420
I mean, if you think it's
tough being an adult, which

00:53:42.420 --> 00:53:43.470
it can be, certainly.

00:53:43.470 --> 00:53:46.980
It's really tough growing
up, as we all know.

00:53:46.980 --> 00:53:49.750
And I think that more
tools, more tools,

00:53:49.750 --> 00:53:52.500
more protocols more tools,
more tools, more protocols,

00:53:52.500 --> 00:53:54.150
more tools.

00:53:54.150 --> 00:53:57.150
But that's obviously
a biased opinion.

00:53:57.150 --> 00:54:00.870
And no one's ever asked me
like, hey, what should we

00:54:00.870 --> 00:54:01.800
be teaching kids?

00:54:01.800 --> 00:54:03.720
But if they ask, as
you can tell, like,

00:54:03.720 --> 00:54:04.930
I'm not going to shut up.

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:08.015
So I think that was
our last question.

00:54:08.015 --> 00:54:09.390
Thank you all for
coming tonight.

00:54:12.150 --> 00:54:14.070
forgive me for going long.

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:17.300
I do want to say
thank you, sincere

00:54:17.300 --> 00:54:19.820
thank you for making your
way out here, especially

00:54:19.820 --> 00:54:20.780
on a weekday night.

00:54:20.780 --> 00:54:23.090
Thanks for your hospitality.

00:54:23.090 --> 00:54:24.980
My incredible team,
I want to thank them.

00:54:24.980 --> 00:54:28.200
And certainly,
last but not least,

00:54:28.200 --> 00:54:30.080
thank you for your
interest in science.

00:54:30.080 --> 00:54:30.890
Thank you.

00:54:30.890 --> 00:54:31.640
Thank you so much.

00:54:31.640 --> 00:54:33.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:35.540 --> 00:54:38.890
[MUSIC PLAYING]

